<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Salm</h1><p class="st-meta">1802 CE – 1811 CE · capital Bocholt</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Napoleonic-era principality in the Münsterland, given to two Salm princes in compensation for lands lost to France.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salm">Salm-Salm</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wild-and-rhinegraviate">Salm-Kyrburg</a> lost their territories in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Under the settlement of 1802 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a>, they jointly received the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocholt">Bocholt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaus">Ahaus</a> from the secularised <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-munster">Prince-Bishopric of Münster</a>, forming the Principality of Salm.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The two princely houses held the state jointly, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salm">Salm-Salm</a> taking two-thirds and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wild-and-rhinegraviate">Salm-Kyrburg</a> one third, and governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocholt">Bocholt</a> and Anholt. The territory was Catholic and agrarian. In 1806 it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> as a sovereign member. The Salm-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrburg">Kyrburg</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-prince-of-salm-kyrburg">Frederick III</a> had been guillotined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1794, and his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iv-prince-of-salm-kyrburg">Frederick IV</a> succeeded him.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In December 1810 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> annexed north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to secure the coast against British trade, and Salm was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1811. In 1815 the territory passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, where the Salm families remained as mediatised landowners.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-salm-1802.jpg" alt="Principality of Salm" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Hierakares · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Salm_principalities_(1798-1811).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1802 CE – 1811 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bocholt</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Constantine of Salm-Salm, Frederick IV of Salm-Kyrburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Salm-Salm, Salm-Kyrburg, Fürstentum Salm</dd></dl></aside></div>
